In PHP, the `==` operator is not type safe, meaning it will perform an automatic type conversion if needed (also known as type juggling). In this case, it converts the string '5' into the integer 5 before doing the comparison. So, it returns TRUE.

Now, let's create a function to mimic PHP's loose comparison (`==`) behavior:

```php
function looseComparison($x, $y)
{
    if (gettype($x) !== gettype($y)) {
        if (is_numeric($x) && is_numeric($y)) {
            return $x == $y;
        }

        return false;
    }

    return $x == $y;
}

// Time to do some tests
var_dump(looseComparison(5, '5'));        // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(5, 5));          // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison('5', '5'));      // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(5, '6'));        // bool(false)
var_dump(looseComparison(5, '5a'));       // bool(false)
var_dump(looseComparison('5a', '5a'));    // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(true, 1));       // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(false, 0));      // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(true, '1'));     // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison('1', 1));        // bool(true)
var_dump(looseComparison(null, 0));       // bool(false)
var_dump(looseComparison(null, false));   // bool(false)
```

Our tests clearly show that the function `looseComparison()` effectively replicates the behavior of the loose comparison operator `==` in PHP. This can be dangerous if we're not careful with the types of the variables we're comparing since PHP freely interchangeable types when values are compared; it's on the programmer's end to ensure that comparisons are type-safe if required. Therefore, if you want a strict comparison where '5' and 5 are considered different, use the `===` operator."}]

